690:
(a) No construction may be initiated within the city for any residential building of any type which:
(1) Employs a gravity tank type, flushometer-valve, or flushometer-tank that uses more than an average of 1.6 gallons of water per flush;
(2) Employs a showerhead that allows a flow of more than an average of 2.5 gallons of water per minute at 60 pounds per square inch of pressure;
(3) Employs a urinal that uses more than an average of 1.0 gallons of water per flush;
(4) Employs a lavatory faucet or lavatory replacement aerator that allows a flow of more than 2.0 gallons of water per minute; or
(5) Employs a kitchen faucet or kitchen replacement aerator that allows a flow of more than 2.5 gallons of water per minute.
(b) There shall be no construction of any commercial building initiated within the city for any commercial building of any type which does not meet the requirements of subsection (a) of this section.
(c) The requirements of subsection (a) of this section shall apply to any residential construction and to any commercial construction which involves the repair or renovation of or addition to any existing building when the repair or renovation of or addition to the existing building includes replacement of toilets or showers or both.
(Code 2004, § 53.03; Ord. No. 316, 5-13-1991)

691:
New construction and the repair or renovation of an existing building shall be exempt when:
(1) The repair or renovation of the existing building does not include the replacement of the plumbing or sewage system servicing toilets, faucets or showerheads within the existing buildings;
(2) When the plumbing or sewage system within the existing building, because of its capacity, design, or installation would not function properly if the toilets, faucets or showerheads required by this chapter were installed;
(3) The system is a well or gravity flow from a spring and is owned privately by an individual for use in the individual's personal residence;
(4) Units to be installed are:
a. Specifically designed for use by the handicapped;
b. Specifically designed to withstand unusual abuse or installation in a penal institution; or
c. Toilets for juveniles; or
(5) The owner, or his agent, of a building undergoing new construction or repair or renovation who is entitled to an exemption, as specified in this section, shall obtain the exemption by applying at the office of the building inspector for the city. A fee, as established by the mayor and council and provided in the city fee schedule, shall be charged for the inspection and issuance of the exemption.
(Code 2004, § 53.04; Ord. No. 316, 5-13-1991)

713:
This article is adopted to address the interest of public safety. As such, the city finds:
(1) There is a need to establish a mechanism to register foreclosed and vacant real property in order to protect property values for all property owners;
(2) Due to the lack of adequate maintenance and security of properties that have been foreclosed or recently subject to transfer of ownership after foreclosure, the property values and quality of life of neighboring properties are negatively impacted;
(3) Improperly maintained and secured foreclosed properties can become a hazard to the health and safety of persons who may come on or near the property, can adversely affect the aesthetic and economic attributes of communities and difficulties often arise in locating the person responsible for the condition of foreclosed real property;
(4) There is a substantial need directly related to the public health, safety and welfare to comprehensively address these concerns through the adoption of the provisions in this chapter; and
(5) The implementation and enforcement of foreclosure and vacant real property registry will circumvent the above recognized problems and will require owners and agents to provide the city with official information for contacting a party responsible for bringing foreclosed and vacant real property into compliance with applicable provisions of the Code of Ordinances of Harlem, Georgia.
(Ord. No. 2002, 5-21-2020)

